Ladybird now keeps a flex container’s items in order-modified document
order for every flex direction, and reverses them only where a line lays
them out from the main-end edge. Blink does the same: its
FlexChildIterator hands out the children sorted by order, and
FlexLayoutAlgorithm applies is_reverse_direction_ only when it places
them. WebKit iterates RenderFlexibleBox’s children with OrderIterator,
and Gecko with CSSOrderAwareFrameIterator, reversing at positioning
through IsMainAxisReversed().

Run in Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5
(Playwright’s build), the test’s six cases (row-reverse, row-reverse
with positive orders, column-reverse, a wrapping row-reverse,
row-reverse in rtl, and row) come out the same in all three, and the
same as Ladybird now.
